  message: "Cross-ref backfill (2026-05-30, deeper-issue investigation): direct read of packages/hypergumbo-lang-mainstream/src/hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/bash.py — likeliest hot spot is _get_enclosing_function (lines 353-362) walking the parent chain for every command node with no memoization, called from extract_edges_from_file's iter_tree pass (line 364). Bash scripts are command-dense (~one command per executable line), so a 200-line script triggers ~200 parent-chain walks per pass. Suggested first probe: cProfile the bash pass on a single ~200-line script before optimizing; if confirmed, memoize via a single Pass-1 traversal that builds node_id → enclosing_function map and look it up in O(1) during Pass 2. Theme-adjacent (not same-mechanic): INV-bikaj-mizug (passes that burn budget on no-edge output)."  # 0c59
- op: discuss  # 3c5b
  at: '2026-06-02T08:02:21Z'  # 3c5b
  by: agent  # 3c5b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3c5b
  clock: 4  # 3c5b
  nonce: 3c5b  # 3c5b
  message: "Pass-32 face: bash analyzer warm-vs-cold spread is 11.2× (F32.1).\n\nWI-tuduf's headline figure is 631ms/file at 26.5s total on 42 files, characterizing the cold-cache path. Pass-32 ran `hypergumbo run` twice on the same source tree back-to-back; the second run produced the strongest single perf delta in the substrate:\n\n```\nbash AR.files_analyzed: 42 -> 42 (identical, no skipped files differ)\nbash AR.duration_ms: 29253ms (frozen) -> 2608ms (fresh, 4h44min later)\nper-file: 696.5ms -> 62.1ms (11.2× speedup)\nwarnings/failed_files/files_skipped: identical [] / [] / 0 on both runs\n```\n\nFor comparison, Python on the same source: 38235ms → 42271ms (0.9× — essentially flat). The 11.2× bash spread is anomalous and isolated to bash among the 84 ARs (other top-duration passes vary <2× across the two runs).\n\n**Interpretation.** WI-tuduf's hypothesis was tree-sitter-bash grammar load OR shellcheck subprocess overhead OR `_get_enclosing_function` parent-chain walk. The 11.2× warm-vs-cold spread implicates **per-run-once initialization**, not per-file work, as the dominant cost. Candidates: tree-sitter-bash grammar module load + parser warmup (paid once per run regardless of file count), OS-level disk cache for the 42 bash files (the second run hits hot fs cache), or shellcheck binary fork overhead amortized across the second-run's already-spawned helper. At 62.1ms warm/file, bash sits 1.5× python's 41ms/file — essentially competitive on the warm path. The \"15× slower than Python\" framing in WI-tuduf's title is the cold-path number; warm-cache, bash is ~1.5× python.\n\n**Implication for WI-tuduf fix scope.** The pass-32 evidence suggests two distinct optimization targets:\n- **Warm path:** already at 62ms/file, ~1.5× python — probably no headroom without an algorithmic change to `_get_enclosing_function`. Worth profiling the warm path specifically to see if the 62ms is dominated by tree-sitter parse or the parent-chain walk.\n- **Cold path:** the 11.2× gap is paid once per `hypergumbo run` (or once per CI job). Likely target: grammar lazy-load or a process-level grammar cache. Saves ~27s on the first run after a cold start; saves nothing on subsequent runs of the same process.\n\nThe 14× / 15× warm-vs-cold spread reading suggests the dominant cost shifts entirely between paths: the parent-chain walk hypothesis explains the warm path; the grammar-load hypothesis explains the cold-vs-warm delta. Both can be true.\n\n**No status change.** Pass-32 supplies the warm-cache perf number that complements WI-tuduf's cold-cache headline, sharpening the fix-scope decision. Probe artifact: `/tmp/p32_outputA2.log` section \"A.14 bash analyzer cold-vs-warm\". Comparison context in section \"A.15 Compare top-5 longest-duration passes both runs\"."  # 3c5b
